{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"<!-- ROLE-MARKER: critic -->\n<!-- GENERIC bundle: minimal protocol sentences only (EXPERIMENTS.md B2).\n     Note: generic loom runs LOOM_MARATHON_CRITIC=off (the per-review LLM\n     critic is an [S] policy); this file exists for critic=auto variants. -->\n# Review Critic\n\nThe user message is a REVIEW CONTRACT (task, attempt, base, candidate,\ndesign). You are cwd-ed into a disposable checkout of the candidate commit.\nReview it against the task's design and acceptance criteria.\n\nWrite `CRITIC-VERDICT.txt` in the current directory; its FIRST line must be\nexactly one of, echoing the contract's values:\n\n    REVIEW attempt=<attempt> commit=<candidate> APPROVED — <reason>\n    REVIEW attempt=<attempt> commit=<candidate> CHANGES-REQUESTED — <reason>\n\nDo not modify the repository or any task state.\n\n\n\n### Multi-Agent Safety Rules\n\nYou are running in a parallel multi-agent environment. Follow these rules strictly:\n\n- **Only modify files directly related to your assigned task** — do not touch files outside your task scope\n- **Never run** `git stash`, `git checkout main`, or `git clean` outside your assigned worktree\n- **Never force-push or reset --hard** without explicit instruction from the user\n- **If you encounter files/changes from another agent**, leave them alone — do not modify, revert, or clean them up\n- **Commit only your changes** — do not stage unrelated modifications with `git add -A` or `git add .`; use specific file paths\n- **If your worktree has unexpected state**, report it via task notes or `loom complete` rather than cleaning it up\n- **Do not switch branches** — you are confined to your assigned worktree branch\n\n\n## User's Initial Request\n\nREVIEW CONTRACT\ntask=MARATHON-2\nattempt=1\nbase=a152118acefde5daa0d42f1edf507de56b34dfec\ncandidate=e2704f33e188e66eba9d4d4225e79b74f8cd9ea1\n\nDESIGN:\n## Summary\n\nMARATHON-2 bootstraps the Huddle cluster runtime at `/app`: a foreground `start.sh` that launches local Redis on `127.0.0.1:6379` and three identical HTTP nodes on `127.0.0.1:8000/8001/8002`, keeps them alive under SIGKILL (HTTP respawn ≤60s; Redis respawn after kill), and exposes a stable `GET /api/health` contract returning `{status:\"ok\", node_id}` with `node_id` ∈ `{0,1,2}` matching ports. This is the foundation every later auth, messaging, WS, IRC, and SPA task assumes: process topology, port map, node identity, and local-only Redis.\n\n## Technical Approach\n\n### Decision\n- **Process model:** One bash supervisor (`/app/start.sh`) stays in the foreground and owns child lifetimes. Children: one `redis-server` + three Node.js HTTP workers. The supervisor polls PIDs (≈1s cadence) and respawns any dead child without exiting.\n- **HTTP runtime:** **Node.js** (v24 present in the environment) using the **stdlib `http` module only** for this task—no npm registry dependency for bootstrap. One shared entrypoint, differentiated solely by `NODE_ID` / `PORT` env vars.\n- **Redis:** System `redis-server` (v7 present), bound to `127.0.0.1:6379`, persistence directory under `/app/data/redis`. Started as a non-daemon child of `start.sh` (no `--daemonize yes`) so the supervisor can track its PID.\n- **Bind address:** HTTP listens on `127.0.0.1` (epic: nodes on `127.0.0.1:8000/8001/8002`). Redis on `127.0.0.1:6379`. No Docker, no remote broker, no cloud services.\n\n### Alternatives considered\n| Alternative | Why rejected |\n|-------------|--------------|\n| systemd / supervisord / Docker Compose | Violates self-contained / no Docker-in-Docker; not available as the harness entrypoint |\n| Python HTTP (stdlib) | Viable, but SPA + WS + IRC gateway later fit one Node process better; env has no FastAPI/Flask |\n| Three separate start scripts | Harness expects a single foreground `/app/start.sh` |\n| Health behind a reverse proxy | Extra moving part; epic requires each node to answer directly on its port |\n\n### Trade-off accepted\nSupervisor simplicity (bash PID loop) over a real process manager: enough for marathon verification (kill + respawn windows), but no fancy backoff/jitter. Fixed ports mean `start.sh` must fail loudly if a protected harness process already owns a port rather than silently remapping.\n\n### Stack conventions fixed here (for siblings)\nThese are **authoritative for subsequent MARATHON designs** unless a later design explicitly argues otherwise:\n\n| Convention | Value |\n|------------|--------|\n| Product | Huddle |\n| App root | `/app` (repo working tree = deployment root) |\n| HTTP runtime | Node.js, CommonJS or ESM; entry `server/index.js` |\n| Node identity | Integer `NODE_ID` ∈ {0,1,2}; `PORT = 8000 + NODE_ID` |\n| Redis | `127.0.0.1:6379`, no password, local-only |\n| Durable shared state | Files under `/app/data/` (schema owned by MARATHON-3+; this task only creates dirs) |\n| Pub/sub | Redis when healthy; fallback owned by MARATHON-19 (not implemented here) |\n| API prefix | `/api/...` |\n| JSON | `Content-Type: application/json; charset=utf-8` |\n\n## Interface Contracts\n\n### C1 — `GET /api/health` (each HTTP node)\n\n| Field | Value |\n|-------|--------|\n| Method / path | `GET /api/health` |\n| Auth | None |\n| Query / body | None; body ignored if present |\n| Success status | `200` |\n| Success body | `{\"status\":\"ok\",\"node_id\":<number>}` |\n| `status` | Exactly the string `\"ok\"` (lowercase) |\n| `node_id` | JSON number `0`, `1`, or `2` — **not** a string |\n| Mapping | Port `8000` → `0`; `8001` → `1`; `8002` → `2` |\n| Headers | `Content-Type: application/json; charset=utf-8` |\n| Redis dependency | **None.** Health MUST succeed even if Redis is down or restarting (required so MARATHON-19 kill tests do not fail health probes). |\n\n**Owner:** Backend (this task). **Consumer:** QA harness, future load balancer checks, SPA may optionally display node identity later.\n\n**Client behavior while slow/failing:**\n- Health is expected to respond in well \n\nYou are cwd-ed into a DISPOSABLE detached checkout of the candidate commit. Review it against the design and acceptance criteria, then write CRITIC-VERDICT.txt in the current directory containing EXACTLY one first line:\nREVIEW attempt=1 commit=e2704f33e188e66eba9d4d4225e79b74f8cd9ea1 APPROVED — <reason>\nor\nREVIEW attempt=1 commit=e2704f33e188e66eba9d4d4225e79b74f8cd9ea1 CHANGES-REQUESTED — <reason>\n\nAddress this request using the lead mode conventions above."}]},"session_id":"5e3b7b08-3227-4a7d-80f8-2dd7bc3e2c93"}
